WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.600
Fund deliveries are not only data.

2
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:07.154
Prospectuses, key information documents and factsheets travel with them.

3
00:00:07.204 --> 00:00:17.780
In this film you see how FundsXML carries documents: as a link, or embedded in the file, how to get them out again with FreeXmlToolkit, and what receivers do with them.

4
00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.835
Why documents in a data format?

5
00:00:20.885 --> 00:00:27.715
Distributors must give investors the current key information document before every purchase, in the right language.

6
00:00:27.765 --> 00:00:31.245
Platforms show prospectuses, annual reports and factsheets.

7
00:00:31.295 --> 00:00:35.014
A fund sold in ten countries quickly has hundreds of documents.

8
00:00:35.064 --> 00:00:42.194
FundsXML lets them travel through the same channel as the data, together with the information which fund they belong to.

9
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:49.839
Documents have their own block at the top level of the file: Documents, next to Funds and the asset master data.

10
00:00:49.889 --> 00:00:59.367
This example delivery carries three documents for the multi-asset fund of our fictitious asset manager: a prospectus, a key information document and a factsheet.

11
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:01.260
Each one is an element Document.

12
00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.927
A document starts with its type.

13
00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:20.076
The schema lists the common ones: the prospectus, the PRIIPs key information document, the factsheet, annual, half-year, quarterly and monthly reports, audit and valuation reports, marketing material, and the sustainability disclosures under S F D R.

14
00:01:20.126 --> 00:01:25.308
A type that is not in the list goes into the element unlisted type, as free text.

15
00:01:25.961 --> 00:01:28.144
You do not need to remember the list.

16
00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:34.358
In FreeXmlToolkit, the documentation of the schema shows all allowed values, right next to the element.

17
00:01:34.408 --> 00:01:39.607
Use a listed type whenever one fits, because receivers sort and filter documents by it.

18
00:01:40.515 --> 00:01:43.960
Then the language, as an I S O code, such as e n or d e.

19
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.645
Whether translations exist.

20
00:01:45.695 --> 00:01:50.077
And the countries where the document is published, also as I S O codes.

21
00:01:50.127 --> 00:01:58.815
A key information document for Austria and Germany in German, and another one for Luxembourg in English, are two separate Document elements.

22
00:01:59.465 --> 00:02:02.241
And then: what does the document belong to?

23
00:02:02.291 --> 00:02:07.170
To the whole fund, to a subfund of an umbrella, or to single share classes.

24
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.339
Each is named by its identifiers.

25
00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:12.034
A prospectus usually belongs to the fund.

26
00:02:12.084 --> 00:02:18.738
A key information document belongs to one or more share classes, here two classes, each with its ISIN.

27
00:02:19.423 --> 00:02:20.522
Then the details.

28
00:02:20.572 --> 00:02:22.345
The name and the file name.

29
00:02:22.395 --> 00:02:24.912
Whether the document may be published.

30
00:02:24.962 --> 00:02:28.694
The format, as a media type, here application slash pdf.

31
00:02:28.744 --> 00:02:31.734
Whether it is signed, digitally or as a scan.

32
00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:37.880
The creation date, and an expiration date, after which the document must no longer be used.

33
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.366
And the size in bytes.

34
00:02:40.516 --> 00:02:46.731
Documents are replaced regularly: a new key information document every year, a new factsheet every month.

35
00:02:46.781 --> 00:02:52.698
The new document has the same type, language and share classes, and a new version and creation date.

36
00:02:52.748 --> 00:02:55.562
Receivers use these to replace the old document.

37
00:02:55.612 --> 00:03:01.231
For a small correction of the same document, the modification date says that something changed.

38
00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:04.661
Not every document is for the public.

39
00:03:04.711 --> 00:03:09.093
Some are only for professional investors, or only for the distributor itself.

40
00:03:09.143 --> 00:03:13.583
Then For Public Usage is false, and Restrictions says in words who may see it.

41
00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:19.627
The document still travels through the same channel, but a receiver must not show it on a public website.

42
00:03:20.977 --> 00:03:22.256
A word on signatures.

43
00:03:22.306 --> 00:03:33.269
The element Signature says whether the document is signed: not at all, digitally, which means the signature sits inside the P D F, or as a scan of a document signed on paper.

44
00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:36.433
This is information for the receiver, not a check.

45
00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:42.446
If the whole delivery has to be protected, the FundsXML file itself can carry an XML signature.

46
00:03:43.696 --> 00:03:44.612
Now the content.

47
00:03:44.662 --> 00:03:45.759
There are two ways.

48
00:03:45.809 --> 00:03:52.764
The prospectus is delivered as a link: the element Document U R L holds the address where the receiver downloads it.

49
00:03:52.814 --> 00:03:56.810
The file stays small, and the document can be updated in one place.

50
00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:01.158
But the link must remain reachable, and the receiver needs access to it.

51
00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:04.481
The key information document is embedded.

52
00:04:04.531 --> 00:04:08.797
The element Binary Data holds the whole P D F, encoded as base64.

53
00:04:08.847 --> 00:04:13.179
The receiver has everything in one file, and nothing can get lost.

54
00:04:13.229 --> 00:04:19.620
The price: base64 makes the content about a third larger, and the file grows with every document.

55
00:04:20.771 --> 00:04:22.023
How does base64 work?

56
00:04:22.073 --> 00:04:28.037
It takes three bytes of the file, twenty-four bits, and splits them into four groups of six bits.

57
00:04:28.087 --> 00:04:33.369
Each group becomes one of sixty-four safe characters: letters, digits, plus and slash.

58
00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:38.391
So three bytes become four characters, which is where the extra third comes from.

59
00:04:38.441 --> 00:04:41.739
Line breaks are allowed and are ignored when decoding.

60
00:04:43.089 --> 00:04:43.808
So which one?

61
00:04:43.858 --> 00:04:49.727
A link suits large documents and documents that change often, if the receiver can reach the address.

62
00:04:49.777 --> 00:04:58.546
Embedding suits deliveries that must be complete on their own, for example for archiving, or when the receiver has no access to the sender's website.

63
00:04:59.896 --> 00:05:08.090
In practice, many senders deliver documents separately: a FundsXML file that contains only ControlData and the Documents block.

64
00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:12.309
It goes out when documents change, not every day with the prices.

65
00:05:12.359 --> 00:05:18.151
That keeps the daily data files small and fast, and documents can follow their own rhythm.

66
00:05:19.401 --> 00:05:21.353
FreeXmlToolkit helps with both.

67
00:05:21.403 --> 00:05:31.685
In the FundsXML panel, the tool Documents lists all documents of the open file: type, language, the share class, whether it is linked or embedded, and its size.

68
00:05:31.735 --> 00:05:35.172
A linked document opens in the browser with one click.

69
00:05:36.124 --> 00:05:40.582
To get an embedded document out, select it and choose Save embedded document.

70
00:05:40.632 --> 00:05:44.972
The tool decodes the base64 content and saves the file under its file name.

71
00:05:45.022 --> 00:05:49.889
It also compares the size with the value in Size in Bytes, and warns if they differ.

72
00:05:49.939 --> 00:05:53.518
Here is the key information document, as the receiver sees it.

73
00:05:54.168 --> 00:05:56.225
The other way round: Embed file.

74
00:05:56.275 --> 00:06:01.623
Choose a P D F, the document type, the language and the share class it belongs to.

75
00:06:01.673 --> 00:06:11.762
The tool adds a new Document element: with the file name, the media type, the size, and the content as base64, wrapped in lines of seventy-six characters.

76
00:06:11.812 --> 00:06:14.987
Validation confirms that the file is still valid.

77
00:06:16.878 --> 00:06:19.242
What does the schema check for documents?

78
00:06:19.292 --> 00:06:25.660
That the base64 content is well formed, that the format and the language are there, and that dates are dates.

79
00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:32.549
What it cannot check: whether the content really is a P D F, whether the size matches, or whether a link still works.

80
00:06:32.599 --> 00:06:37.024
Those are business checks, done by the tool, or by the receiver's own rules.

81
00:06:38.374 --> 00:06:40.897
What does a receiver do with the documents?

82
00:06:40.947 --> 00:06:49.813
It reads the list, matches each document to its share classes by ISIN, stores the file, and replaces the older version of the same type and language.

83
00:06:49.863 --> 00:06:53.403
Expiration dates tell it when a document must be taken down.

84
00:06:53.453 --> 00:06:58.729
Then the platform or the distributor's system can show the current document to investors.

85
00:07:00.079 --> 00:07:03.376
Without the tool, the same works with standard means.

86
00:07:03.426 --> 00:07:11.397
Select the Binary Data element with X Path, and decode it with base64 on the command line, or in a few lines of Python or Java.

87
00:07:11.447 --> 00:07:19.228
For large deliveries with many documents, read the file as a stream, so that the documents do not all sit in memory at once.

88
00:07:20.578 --> 00:07:21.236
A few tips.

89
00:07:21.286 --> 00:07:25.486
Keep the size in bytes correct; receivers use it as a first check.

90
00:07:25.536 --> 00:07:30.895
Set an expiration date on documents that are replaced regularly, such as factsheets.

91
00:07:30.945 --> 00:07:33.664
Mark documents that are not for the public.

92
00:07:33.714 --> 00:07:35.853
Deliver one document per language.

93
00:07:35.903 --> 00:07:41.649
And note: FundsXML has no checksum for documents yet; that would be a good change request.

94
00:07:42.999 --> 00:07:49.875
That was documents in FundsXML: linked or embedded, how to get them in and out, and what receivers do with them.

95
00:07:49.925 --> 00:07:54.143
In the next film, you validate a delivery and check its data quality.

96
00:07:54.193 --> 00:07:55.380
Thanks for watching.
